==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|grandiose}}, from {{der|en|it|grandioso}}, from {{der|en|la|grandis|t=great, grand}} ({{cog|en|grand}}). Possibly from {{suf|fr|grand|ose}}, though to be debated. {{doublet|en|grandioso}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɹæn.diˈəʊs/|/ˈɡɹæn.di.əʊs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-grandiose.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊs|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[large|Large]] and [[impressive]], in [[size]], [[scope]] or [[extent]].
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2019-03-06|author=Nalini Mohabir|url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2019/mar/06/renaming-cook-islands-independence-maori|title=Renaming the Cook Islands would be a vital step towards true independence|work=The Guardian|passage=Independence does not need to be a '''grandiose''' process of disconnection and severing ties.}}
# [[pompous|Pompous]] or [[pretentious]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 December 29|author=Stephen Roberts|title=Stories and facts behind railway plaques: Didcot (1932)|journal=RAIL|issue=947|page=60|text=There is a station here, of course, opened as Didcot in June 1844 and renamed as the '''more grandiose'''-sounding Didcot Parkway in July 1985.}}

